<h2>What buyers still want to know</h2>
</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is the 4100 really enough, or should I spend more?</summary><p class="faq-answer">For most people, it is enough. The useful features are already there: timer, pressure sensing, good cleaning, and simple ownership.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>How long does the battery last?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Battery life varies by use, but the 4100 is designed to go many days between charges. For most buyers, charging is not a major ownership issue.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Does an electric toothbrush actually work better than a manual brush?</summary><p class="faq-answer">It can, especially if it helps you brush for the right amount of time and avoid pressing too hard. The habit improvement is often the real benefit.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Can I use cheaper off-brand replacement heads?</summary><p class="faq-answer">You can, but quality varies. Poor replacement heads may feel rougher, fit worse, or reduce the cleaning experience.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Do I need pressure sensing?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Yes, it is one of the features worth having. Many people brush too hard, and pressure sensing helps reduce gum irritation and enamel wear.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Do I need an app-connected toothbrush?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Most people do not. App coaching can help some users, but it is not necessary for a strong daily brushing routine.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How often should I replace the brush head?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A common rule is about every three months, or sooner if bristles look worn. Worn heads clean less effectively.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Is Sonicare better than Oral-B?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Neither is automatically better for everyone. Sonicare often feels smoother and quieter, while Oral-B’s round head feels more mechanical and direct.</p></details>
</div>
